§ `VerifyConstantsReadThroughFixture`

-- Controls for verify-constants.l4 (smucclaw/l4-ide#1095): only a body that
-- IS the literal is a constant. A rule that reads through a constant keeps
-- whatever finding the constant gives it, and so does a contradiction written
-- out.

GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the waiver was signed` MEANS FALSE

GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the rule is in force` IF TRUE

-- FALSE either way, but not the literal itself, so still `unsat`: the
-- exemption is for a body that IS the literal, not for any body without atoms.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `false either way` IF FALSE OR FALSE

-- A contradiction written out: still `unsat`.
GIVEN x IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `nobody can satisfy this` x IF
    x AND NOT x

-- Reads through the constant FALSE, so it is TRUE for no input.
GIVEN y IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `needs the waiver` y IF
    y AND `the waiver was signed`

-- Reads through the constant TRUE, so `x` cannot change the answer.
GIVEN x IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `x cannot matter` x IF
    x OR `the rule is in force`

-- A rule whose meaning is FALSE once it is read through, but which is not
-- itself a constant: its body is a call. Reading through it says so.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the waiver, as recorded` MEANS `the waiver was signed`

GIVEN y IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `needs the recorded waiver` y IF
    y AND `the waiver, as recorded`

-- Reads through both constants, and is TRUE for no input. The FALSE one can
-- be why; the TRUE one cannot, and is not named.
GIVEN y IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `needs the waiver while in force` y IF
    y AND `the waiver was signed` AND `the rule is in force`

-- Reads through both, and is TRUE for every input. Only the TRUE one is named
-- for that; the FALSE one is a dead branch, which it is named for.
GIVEN x IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `x cannot matter, whatever the waiver` x IF
    x OR `the rule is in force` OR `the waiver was signed`
